{"timestamp":"2026-09-22T20:36:37.568Z","level":"info","event":"provider.together.response","fields":{"provider":"together","endpoint":"/chat/completions","response":{"httpStatus":200,"contentType":"text/event-stream","providerRequestId":"3b1dc259-aws_ew1","model":"moonshotai/Kimi-K3","text":"{\n  \"schemaVersion\": \"outline-v1\",\n  \"targetDurationSeconds\": 180,\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"hook\",\n      \"title\": \"Two Very Different Words for One Idea\",\n      \"description\": \"Pose a thought-provoking question to spark curiosity: imagine someone tells you that righteousness, justice, and generosity are all the same word in Hebrew. How would that change the way you think about your faith and your money? This opening sets the stage for exploring Tzedakah and the twin manifestations of righteousness that will unfold throughout the lesson.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7355-b1db-e3dafa507991\",\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7970-8683-661a513bb105\"\n      ],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 20,\n      \"framingNote\": \"This is a generated framing device designed to introduce the theme of righteousness as both a gift and a practical harvest, and to prime learners for the Hebrew concept of Tzedakah.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"concept\",\n      \"title\": \"Two Manifestations of Righteousness\",\n      \"description\": \"Teach the core distinction between righteousness as a gift (positional, received by grace through faith, fixed and unchangeable) and the harvest of righteousness (practical, dynamic, multiplied through visible works). Explain why both must be visible and active in a believer's life, and warn against the dangerous doctrinal imbalance of neglecting either one.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7355-b1db-e3dafa507991\"\n      ],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\n        \"c963b0b0-1108-7ea7-940d-01123d9cc020\",\n        \"7af22950-46db-75ef-a410-7afe128a90aa\",\n        \"d0c0e6db-3dc0-7132-80b1-b30a86bfb826\"\n      ],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 30\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"concept\",\n      \"title\": \"Tzedakah: Three Ramifications of One Hebrew Word\",\n      \"description\": \"Introduce the Hebrew word Tzedakah and unpack its three inseparable ramifications: personal morality (internal rectitude, refusing to lie or steal), justice (struct","textChunks":["{\n  \"schemaVersion\": \"outline-v1\",\n  \"targetDurationSeconds\": 180,\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"hook\",\n      \"title\": \"Two Very Different Words for One Idea\",\n      \"description\": \"Pose a thought-provoking question to spark curiosity: imagine someone tells you that righteousness, justice, and generosity are all the same word in Hebrew. How would that change the way you think about your faith and your money? This opening sets the stage for exploring Tzedakah and the twin manifestations of righteousness that will unfold throughout the lesson.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7355-b1db-e3dafa507991\",\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7970-8683-661a513bb105\"\n      ],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 20,\n      \"framingNote\": \"This is a generated framing device designed to introduce the theme of righteousness as both a gift and a practical harvest, and to prime learners for the Hebrew concept of Tzedakah.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"concept\",\n      \"title\": \"Two Manifestations of Righteousness\",\n      \"description\": \"Teach the core distinction between righteousness as a gift (positional, received by grace through faith, fixed and unchangeable) and the harvest of righteousness (practical, dynamic, multiplied through visible works). Explain why both must be visible and active in a believer's life, and warn against the dangerous doctrinal imbalance of neglecting either one.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7355-b1db-e3dafa507991\"\n      ],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\n        \"c963b0b0-1108-7ea7-940d-01123d9cc020\",\n        \"7af22950-46db-75ef-a410-7afe128a90aa\",\n        \"d0c0e6db-3dc0-7132-80b1-b30a86bfb826\"\n      ],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 30\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"concept\",\n      \"title\": \"Tzedakah: Three Ramifications of One Hebrew Word\",\n      \"description\": \"Introduce the Hebrew word Tzedakah and unpack its three inseparable ramifications: personal morality (internal rectitude, refusing to lie or steal), justice (struct","ural uprightness in societal, legal, and business dealings), and charity/generosity (giving to the poor as a matter of justice, not optional benevolence). Show how each ramification applies directly to daily conduct.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7970-8683-661a513bb105\"\n      ],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\n        \"06034497-9561-73ab-9a48-7e0b4dd07526\",\n        \"74a62c36-c225-74cd-ab8b-4c70bfa21c0c\",\n        \"fe3027da-43fa-7c54-abee-645434318ebe\",\n        \"49c29e8f-988b-7974-8e1c-98507766b8dc\"\n      ],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 30\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"example\",\n      \"title\": \"Kingdom Economics in Action\",\n      \"description\": \"Walk through several concrete Kingdom Economics practices drawn from Biblical law: returning a poor debtor's cloak before sundown (fairness over legal rights), periodic debt cancellation every seven years (the Jubilee), leaving gleanings at the edges of fields for the poor and the stranger, prompt payment of workers, and refusing to exploit someone's desperation for profit. For each practice, explain how it builds protection for the vulnerable directly into economic life.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7b8d-9e03-abc39630db01\"\n      ],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\n        \"b29eff38-6707-7ec8-9cc2-3acf8b734309\",\n        \"23d224bd-1662-7e5a-93b1-c26aa4216531\",\n        \"f1f0308a-70b9-7c61-bf74-93a6bf2ad067\",\n        \"a8294542-be65-7173-89e6-421f1913a466\",\n        \"9192540a-67d8-7cd6-8b2a-7c756cd244d9\",\n        \"b2b17cf5-d046-7435-b026-b9e3ddb02152\"\n      ],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 35\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"analogy\",\n      \"title\": \"Power Produces the Miracle; Wisdom Sustains It\",\n      \"description\": \"Use the Jethro narrative (Exodus 18) and the Mark 5 resurrection account to illustrate the critical difference between the power of God and the wisdom of God. Moses had miraculous power but was burning out until Jethro taught him structural delegation. Jesus raised Jairus's daughter by supernatural power"," but immediately commanded that she be given food—practical wisdom to sustain what power had produced. Predict what happens when wisdom does not follow power: burnout, loss, and destruction of what was built.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7a1a-9412-52d954644606\"\n      ],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\n        \"afe140a5-8141-7709-8e11-8dfb41acef12\",\n        \"44fdb66c-c888-7231-9e57-293f33c4efea\",\n        \"2f2a6065-ab09-75d0-b83d-f382b614cf54\"\n      ],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 25\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"concept\",\n      \"title\": \"Five Pillars of Financial Stewardship and the 50/30/20 Rule\",\n      \"description\": \"Present the five pillars of financial stewardship: budgeting and tracking, protection (insurance and emergency funds), savings, investment, and debt management. Then demonstrate the 50/30/20 allocation rule—50% for needs, 30% for wants, 20% for savings and investment—and walk through a sample budget calculation so learners can see how to apply the formula to a real income figure.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7239-afef-9a0413bb4e1c\"\n      ],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\n        \"f95b27d2-c234-738f-87ee-cf404117fd5a\",\n        \"d8d10398-fc31-7207-8067-7d205b09e480\",\n        \"dd8e865f-a031-7bdc-8f60-67cddffe293f\",\n        \"19c878fa-239b-717b-b639-679449af7ad3\",\n        \"7a32b18c-fe32-715f-b6f2-3ef07a0836cf\",\n        \"66e311a8-add0-7a1d-8ef6-093444c88a60\",\n        \"09df5ee4-b668-7289-9457-76279f00717b\"\n      ],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 25\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"summary\",\n      \"title\": \"Faithfulness Is the Standard, Not the Amount\",\n      \"description\": \"Close by drawing together the Parable of the Talents (Matthew 25) with the lesson's major threads. Compare the outcomes of the servants: the five-talent and two-talent servants grew what they were given, while the one-talent servant was condemned not because he had less, but because he showed zero growth and made no interest. Summarize the key takeaways: righteousness is bot","h a gift and a harvest, Tzedakah demands moral integrity, justice, and generosity, Kingdom Economics protects the vulnerable, wisdom must follow power, and faithful stewardship requires deliberate systems across all five pillars.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7bc6-ae8b-1812d9d98dc6\",\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7355-b1db-e3dafa507991\",\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7970-8683-661a513bb105\",\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7b8d-9e03-abc39630db01\",\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7a1a-9412-52d954644606\",\n        \"01a0cace-ecd7-7239-afef-9a0413bb4e1c\"\n      ],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\n        \"49ec6ab8-4041-757d-8594-ceb5170b4b8e\"\n      ],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 15\n    }\n  ]\n}"],"textChunksTruncated":false,"textCharacters":6674,"reasoningCharacters":0,"finishReason":"stop","inputTokens":"[REDACTED]","outputTokens":"[REDACTED]"},"retries":0,"latencyMs":31104,"service":"pipeline-worker","correlationId":"01a0cad5-2f4e-757e-86e5-21fdf7abf048"}}
